 and Ohio: Cumberland \u2013 Pittsburgh (ex-P&C)", "id": "bo-cumberland-pittsburgh", "color": "#00447C", "mode": "intercity_rail", "avg_speed_kmh": 55, "speed_source": "estimated: typical B&O mainline service of the era; unsourced", "segments": [{"start": 1872, "end": 1971, "headway_min": 150, "note": "Leased by the B&O in 1872, giving it a direct Cumberland-Pittsburgh route (rather than relying on trackage rights) as part of its Pittsburgh-Chicago corridor - later used by named trains like the Capitol Limited (see baltimore_and_ohio_capitol_limited.geojson). Connellsville became a major B&O division point, keeping a fleet of helper locomotives on hand around the clock to push heavy coal and coke trains over the mountain grades. Passenger service ended with Amtrak's creation May 1, 1971. Frequency estimated, unsourced.", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baltimore_and_Ohio_Railroad"]}]}, "geometry": {"type": "LineString", "coordinates": [[-78.762383, 39.6526498], [-79.3567016, 39.8128548], [-79.6023538722341, 40.026764431377096], [-79.59665487761755, 40.03547323488812], [-79.59808194485527, 40.04025715244574], [-79.61154022312216, 40.04797807815837], [-79.62364039135511, 40.04678716496239], [-79.63128541965636, 40.03915435495269], [-79.63711448471328, 40.03737342235509], [-79.6660840740064, 40.050655311214484], [-79.67586425048083, 40.05287631595108], [-79.6833184735662, 40.06107718338067], [-79.68829355943481, 40.06169519555856], [-79.69477853628919, 40.052776415126324], [-79.69889060256884, 40.053032431625184], [-79.70233877096065, 40.06124127452387], [-79.71113594737292, 40.06395325984141], [-79.7253343136225, 40.07356311981746], [-79.7132882175628, 40.08024191941897], [-79.71090725613594, 40.08554779517223